Last I checked, the tilesets for NetHack are just stored in plain
old .png files, so any standard image editor should work (possibly in
conjunction with a separate format converter).  It's been a long time
since I used DOS, but IIRC there were some halfway decent ones out
there as freeware, provided you don't expect advanced photo-editing
features (layers and filters and whatnot).  For the format conversion
aspect of things, there was a good one called simply "display" that
supported approximately every image format ever devised (up to that
point in time, circa 1997).  Thus you can use an editor that only
supports a more limited repertoire of formats (e.g., a lot of DOS-
based graphics software saved as PCX, so if you use one of those, you
can use display to convert the results to PNG format).

The larger problem you may run into is that most DOS-based image
editing software did not support TrueColor (the kind of color we now
take for granted, what Gimp calls RGB mode).  Often you were limited
to a 256-slot palette.  You could change what colors were in the
palette, but you could only use 256 of them total in any one image.
The main reason for this limitation was that it was all the PC video
hardware of the day could really handle anyway -- indeed, a lot of
people were still stuck in 16-color mode.

Limits on display resolution could also be an issue.  The highest-res
mode that was at all commonly supported (by PC hardware by the time
almost all software development had switched over from DOS to Windows
as a target platform) was 640x480, and you could usually only get that
with a much more limited color palette (typically, 4-bit color).  All
of the tilesets I've seen for Nethack are stored in one big image, and
the only one that's smaller than 640x480 is the one designed for 12x16
tiles.  Then again, if there's a tile-based DOS version of NetHack, it
probably uses small tiles, so that may not be a big problem.

Talking about all this brings back memories of running FracTint on a
386 SX.


